    "summary": "The Somali Council of Ministers has approved the appointment of four new members to the Supreme Court, a step aimed at strengthening judicial independence and the rule of law.",
    "content": "Mogadishu – The Council of Ministers of the Federal Republic of Somalia, chaired by Deputy Prime Minister Salah Jama, has overwhelmingly approved the appointment of four new members to the Supreme Court, as outlined in Article 145 of the 2026 Federal Constitution.\n\nThe approval followed a formal proposal from the Ministry of Justice and Constitutional Affairs. This decision marks a key move in the ongoing efforts to reinforce the independence of the judiciary and build robust legal institutions. For the Somali diaspora, many of whom closely follow governance reforms in their homeland, this development signals progress in the rule of law and may foster greater confidence in Somalia's judicial system.\n\nThe process of appointing Supreme Court justices is central to ensuring fair and impartial administration of justice. The new appointees are expected to take their seats following the issuance of a presidential decree, as required by law. Their work will involve adjudicating critical cases and interpreting the constitution, directly impacting the lives of Somali citizens at home and abroad.\n\nThe Council of Ministers' swift approval underscores the government's commitment to filling judicial vacancies, which have been a concern for legal experts and civil society. This step is part of broader institutional reforms aimed at stabilizing Somalia's political and legal landscape ahead of upcoming elections.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Dalsan here: https://radiodalsan.com/2026/07/23/golaha-wasiirrada-oo-ansixiyay-magacaabista-4-xubnood-oo-garsoorka-sare/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=golaha-wasiirrada-oo-ansixiyay-magacaabista-4-xubnood-oo-garsoorka-sare",
